response:
Yes, sure!  In general, you’ll want to have a large set of elements, and the loop will have a fixed block of code to run repeatedly.  
A common scenario is to iterate over the elements of a list, or the elements of a set, or to do something with an array.  
You can break out of a loop by adding a break keyword inside the loop.  Do you want to talk about the syntax for for loops in C?  The general syntax for a for loop consists of 

for (statement) {
  statement;
  do something;
}

In the for loop, you can add an increment or decrement operator to the statement section, and you can also include the loop counter variable outside of the for loop, outside of the increment or decrement operator, so that it can be used in subsequent statements.  For example, you could do:     
for (i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
  x = y;
  y = x++;
}

